  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday: Clouds will clear out for much of Wednesday as drier\nair filters into the Inland Northwest. Clear skies and light \nwinds early Wednesday night will potentially allow low \ntemperatures across the northern valleys to plummet into the \n30s. Uncertainty remains as to how much the temperatures will be\nable to drop before more moisture moves over the area and cloud\ncover rolls in, limiting further radiative cooling. \n\nThursday through Monday: Temperatures will trend warmer heading\ninto the weekend as an upper- level ridge builds across the \nwestern US. Zonal flow will persist initially over the Inland \nNorthwest Thursday and Friday with a weak weather disturbance\nbringing a small chance (20%) for light rain showers across the \nNorth Cascades Thursday into Friday. Elsewhere, this \ndisturbance will primarily result in increased cloud cover. \nEnsembles are in good agreement for the ridge to amplify and become\nestablished over the Pacific Northwest this weekend, initiating\na prolonged period of dry weather and above normal \ntemperatures. Beyond Monday, ensemble consensus favors \npersistent upper-level ridging remaining in place through the \nfirst week of October. Great pumpkin patch weather lies ahead! \n/Fewkes",
